ge groups that may correlate strongly with credit card application success.\ndf['Age_Category_Bin'] = pd.cut(df['Age'], bins=[0, 25, 35, 50, 65, 100], labels=['Youth', 'Young Adult', 'Middle-aged', 'Senior', 'Elder'])\n\n# Apply one-hot encoding for downstream modeling if required.\ndf = pd.get_dummies(df, columns=['Age_Category_Bin'], prefix='AgeCat')\nend\n\npython\n# Employment_Inconsistency\n# Feature description: Calculate the inconsistency in employment history using the ratio of YearsEmployed to Employment status (Employed).\n# Usefulness: Individuals with low years employed but marked as employed might have unstable employment patterns, which can be a useful indicator for creditworthiness.\ndf['Employment_Inconsistency'] = df['YearsEmployed'] / (df['Employed'] + 0.5)  # Adding 0.5 to avoid division by zero for unemployed individuals.\nend\n\npython\n# Debt_to_CreditScore\n# Feature description: Calculate the ratio of Debt to CreditScore to measure debt relative to creditworthiness.\n# Usefulness: Those with low credit scores and high debt may struggle with credit management, providing insight into likelihood of credit card application success.\ndf['Debt_to_CreditScore'] = df['Debt'] / (df['CreditScore'] + 1)  # Adding 1 to avoid division by zero for individuals with CreditScore of 0.\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column DriversLicense is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['DriversLicense'], inplace=True)\n# DriversLicense is dropped because it provides minimal variability and relevance to credit card applications, as it is unlikely to significantly impact predictive modeling of success.\n\n# Explanation why the column ZipCode is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['ZipCode'], inplace=True)\n# ZipCode is dropped due to its high-cardinality nature and lack of direct relevance to creditworthiness prediction.\nend", "Here are three proposed next steps for feature engineering to improve predictions for the downstream task of determining credit card application success:\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Age Group Binning\npython\n# age_group\n# Feature description: Bin the Age feature into categorical age groups (e.g., Teenager, YoungAdult, MiddleAged, Senior).\n# Usefulness: Age can have a non-linear relationship with credit card application success. Binning can capture patterns within distinct age brackets, especially for younger or older applicants who may behave differently in financial decisions.\nbins = [0, 18, 30, 50, 65, 100]\nlabels = ['Teenager', 'YoungAdult', 'MiddleAged', 'Senior', 'Elderly']\ndf['age_group'] = pd.cut(df['Age'], bins=bins, labels=labels, right=False)\n# Apply one-hot encoding for compatibility with downstream models\ndf = pd.concat([df, pd.get_dummies(df['age_group'], prefix='age_group')], axis=1)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Employment-to-Age Ratio\npython\n# employment_to_age_ratio\n# Feature description: Calculate the ratio of YearsEmployed to Age.\n# Usefulness: This feature quantifies the proportion of a person's life they have spent working, which could indicate financial stability and job experience, both important factors in creditworthiness.\ndf['employment_to_age_ratio'] = df['YearsEmployed'] / (df['Age'] + 1e-5)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Interaction Between Credit Score and Prior Default\npython\n# credit_score_prior_default_interaction\n# Feature description: Combine CreditScore and PriorDefault into a single feature by multiplying them.\n# Usefulness: Prior defaults may nullify the benefit of a good credit score. This interaction feature can help model the compounded effect of both variables in predicting creditworthiness.\ndf['credit_score_prior_default_interaction'] = df['CreditScore'] * (1 - df['PriorDefault'])\nend\n\n---\n\n### Columns to Drop\npython\n# Explanation why the column total_burden is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['total_burden'], inplace=True)\n# Reason: The column total_burden, while combining Debt and YearsEmployed, was found redundant as we engineered more fine-grained features like employment_to_age_ratio.\n\n# Explanation why the column debt_to_income_ratio is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['debt_to_income_ratio'], inplace=True)\n# Reason: The feature debt_to_income_ratio has extreme variability (very high range and unstable distribution) and was ultimately adding noise rather than contributing predictive value.\nend", "Here are three intuitive and effective feature engineering steps to improve predictions for credit card application success, along with reasoning and explanations provided in the dataset's context:\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Debt-to-CreditScore Ratio\npython\n# debt_to_creditscore_ratio\n# Feature description: Calculate the ratio of Debt to CreditScore for each individual.\n# Usefulness: This feature captures how well an individual's credit score corresponds to their debt levels. A high debt relative to credit score can indicate poor financial management and increased risk, which is crucial for assessing creditworthiness.\ndf['debt_to_creditscore_ratio'] = df['Debt'] / (df['CreditScore'] + 1e-5)  # Adding 1e-5 to avoid division by zero.\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Employment Stability Score\npython\n# employment_stability_score\n# Feature description: Calculate the ratio of YearsEmployed to Age for each individual.\n# Usefulness: This feature reflects how much of an individual's life has been spent in stable employment. People with a higher employment-to-age ratio might demonstrate greater financial stability, which can positively influence creditworthiness.\ndf['employment_stability_score'] = df['YearsEmployed'] / (df['Age'] + 1e-5)  # Adding 1e-5 to avoid division by zero.\nend\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Interaction Between Income and Citizenship\npython\n# income_citizenship_interaction\n# Feature description: Capture the interaction between Income and Citizenship by creating a categorical feature to encode financial stability across citizenship types.\n# Usefulness: People with higher incomes may have different creditworthiness assessments based on their citizenship status (e.g., temporary vs. by birth). This feature helps model such patterns explicitly.\ndf['income_citizenship_interaction'] = df['Citizen'] + '_' + pd.cut(df['Income'], bins=[-1, 0, 5000, 100000, float('inf')], labels=['NoIncome', 'LowIncome', 'MidIncome', 'HighIncome']).astype(str)\n\n# Apply one-hot encoding to make this categorical interaction usable in downstream numeric models.\ndf = pd.get_dummies(df, columns=['income_citizenship_interaction'], prefix='income_citizen')\nend\n\n---\n\n### Columns to Drop\npython\n# Explanation why the column Ethnicity is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['Ethnicity'], inplace=True)\n# Reason: Ethnicity introduces potential ethical concerns and does not provide strong direct relevance to financial decision-making in a creditworthiness context.\n\n# Explanation why the column DriversLicense is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['DriversLicense'], inplace=True)\n# Reason: DriversLicense is unlikely to provide significant predictive value since possession of a driver's license has little correlation with financial health.\n\n# Explanation why the column total_burden is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['total_burden'], inplace=True)\n# Reason: The column total_burden combines Debt and YearsEmployed, but we have engineered more meaningful features (e.g., employment_stability_score and debt_to_creditscore_ratio) that capture this relationship more effectively.\nend", "python\n# Age_Income_Interaction\n# Feature description: Multiply Age and Income to create an interaction term representing the combined effect of age and earning potential.\n# Usefulness: Younger individuals earning higher incomes may demonstrate financial promise, while older individuals with lower incomes might face financial constraints. This interaction captures such nuances.\ndf['Age_Income_Interaction'] = df['Age'] * df['Income']\nend\n\npython\n# Debt_Per_Year_Employed\n# Feature description: Calculate the ratio of Debt to YearsEmployed, representing the debt burden proportional to employment history.\n# Usefulness: People with high debt relative to their employment duration may struggle with financial management, making this feature a strong predictor of creditworthiness.\ndf['Debt_Per_Year_Employed'] = df['Debt'] / (df['YearsEmployed'] + 1e-5)  # Adding 1e-5 to avoid division by zero.\nend\n\npython\n# Ethnicity_Employment_Interaction\n# Feature description: Create an interaction term between Ethnicity and Employment status to capture cultural or societal employment trends.\n# Usefulness: Certain ethnic groups may face systemic challenges or differences in employment stability, which could influence their credit card application success.\ndf['Ethnicity_Employment_Interaction'] = df['Ethnicity'].astype(str) + '_' + df['Employed'].astype(str)\n\n# Apply label encoding to convert this categorical interaction to numerical values.\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import LabelEncoder\nlabel_encoder = LabelEncoder()\ndf['Ethnicity_Employment_Interaction'] = label_encoder.fit_transform(df['Ethnicity_Employment_Interaction'])\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column DriversLicense is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['DriversLicense'], inplace=True)\n# Reason: DriversLicense likely has minimal variability or relevance to creditworthiness and does not provide significant predictive value.\n\n# Explanation why the column Industry is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['Industry'], inplace=True)\n# Reason: Industry by itself does not directly determine creditworthiness; however, its effect has been captured through the engineered interaction features.\nend", "python\n# Age_Binned\n# Feature description: Create age groups by binning the Age feature into discrete categories (e.g., Teen, YoungAdult, MiddleAged, Senior).\n# Usefulness: Age can affect financial behavior and risk levels. Grouping into bins captures non-linear relationships and general trends associated with creditworthiness.\nbins = [0, 18, 30, 50, 65, 100]\nlabels = ['Teen', 'YoungAdult', 'MiddleAged', 'Senior', 'Elderly']\ndf['Age_Binned'] = pd.cut(df['Age'], bins=bins, labels=labels)\n# Apply one-hot encoding for categorical models.\ndf = pd.get_dummies(df, columns=['Age_Binned'], prefix='AgeGroup')\nend\n\npython\n# Debt_to_CreditScore_Ratio\n# Feature description: Calculate the ratio of Debt to CreditScore for individuals.\n# Usefulness: This captures how well a person's debt corresponds to their creditworthiness. A high ratio could indicate financial mismanagement, which is important for predicting credit card approval success.\ndf['Debt_to_CreditScore_Ratio'] = df['Debt'] / (df['CreditScore'] + 1e-5)  # Add small constant to avoid division by zero.\nend\n\npython\n# Employment_and_Citizenship_Interaction\n# Feature description: Create an interaction between employment status (Employed) and citizenship type.\n# Usefulness: People who are employed and citizens by birth might have better financial and job stability, whereas temporary citizens may have a different profile, impacting creditworthiness.\ndf['Employment_and_Citizenship_Interaction'] = df['Employed'].astype(str) + '_' + df['Citizen']\n# Apply label encoding to convert this interaction to numerical values for modeling.\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import LabelEncoder\nlabel_encoder = LabelEncoder()\ndf['Employment_and_Citizenship_Interaction'] = label_encoder.fit_transform(df['Employment_and_Citizenship_Interaction'])\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column DriversLicense is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['DriversLicense'], inplace=True)\n# DriversLicense lacks variability or clear relevance to creditworthiness. It does not bring value to the predictive model.\n\n# Explanation why the column ZipCode is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['ZipCode'], inplace=True)\n# ZipCode is a high-cardinality feature with minimal relevance to financial behavior or creditworthiness. It introduces noise rather than improving predictions.\nend", "python\n# Age_Income_Ratio\n# Feature description: Calculate the ratio of Income to Age for each individual. Represents financial earning potential relative to age.\n# Usefulness: Applicants with higher income relative to their age may demonstrate better financial stability and maturity, which is useful for predicting creditworthiness.\ndf['Age_Income_Ratio'] = df['Income'] / (df['Age'] + 1e-5)\nend\n\npython\n# Debt_Per_Employed_Year\n# Feature description: Calculate the ratio of Debt to YearsEmployed for each individual.\n# Usefulness: Represents the financial burden of debt relative to an individual’s employment years, giving insight into their financial management over their working history.\ndf['Debt_Per_Employed_Year'] = df['Debt'] / (df['YearsEmployed'] + 1e-5)\nend\n\npython\n# Employment_Age_Stability\n# Feature description: Calculate the ratio of YearsEmployed to Age.\n# Usefulness: Reflects how much of a person’s life has been spent in employment. Higher values indicate financial stability and experience, likely correlated with successful credit card applications.\ndf['Employment_Age_Stability'] = df['YearsEmployed'] / (df['Age'] + 1e-5)\nend\n\npython\n# Explanation why the column DriversLicense is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['DriversLicense'], inplace=True)\n# Reason: DriversLicense does not provide meaningful information related to financial health or creditworthiness.\n\n# Explanation why the column Industry is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['Industry'], inplace=True)\n# Reason: While industry may influence job stability, its effect is better captured in the industry_employment_score or other engineered features like Employment_Age_Stability.\n\n# Explanation why the column Ethnicity is dropped\ndf.drop(columns=['Ethnicity'], inplace=True)\n# Reason: Ethnicity introduces potential ethical concerns and does not provide clear predictive value for assessing creditworthiness.\nend"]###,
